# newframework

A long-term learning project: an original, compact C++23 tensor,
automatic-differentiation, and neural-network framework. Conceptual destination:

**frontend → scheduler → lowering/code generation → execution**

Learn tensor layouts, numerical kernels and floating-point behavior, reverse-mode
VJPs, training, lazy graphs, scheduling, memory planning, and execution through
small, tested steps. Correctness and explicit contracts precede performance;
reference kernels stay when optimized alternatives arrive.

## Current scope

Only an owning, dense, contiguous, row-major CPU `float32` Tensor, checked
indexing, metadata-only reshape, and exact-shape addition. Standard-library
containers provide RAII and value semantics; no external dependencies or testing
frameworks. No raw owning pointers or manual allocation management.

Requires CMake 3.20+ and a compiler accepting C++23 (Clang or GCC); C++26 is not
required. `float` must be IEEE 754 binary32, verified at compile time.

## Roadmap (not scaffolding)

0. Repository and build foundation — ...
1. Dense CPU Tensor and reference operations — initial narrow slice here;
   broader operations, broadcasting, strides/views come later.
2. Eager reverse-mode autodiff using VJPs.
3. Modules, losses, optimizers, and training.
4. Lazy operation graph and small IR.
5. Scheduling, fusion, and CPU code generation.
6. Memory planning and measured performance work, keeping reference paths.
7. A second execution backend, eventually one accelerator.
8. Stable downstream integration boundary and observability.

Deferred: broadcasting, views, autograd, expression templates, generic/multiple
dtypes, GPU support, sparse tensors, distributed execution, plugins, and
optimizations. No abstractions for hypothetical requirements.

## Conceptual references

Specifications and theory inform contracts, not copied implementations:

- [Deep Learning Book](https://www.deeplearningbook.org/), Chapters 4 and 6:
  numerical computation and feedforward networks.
- [Baydin et al., Automatic Differentiation in Machine Learning](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v18/17-468.html): AD and reverse mode.
- [Python Array API Standard](https://data-apis.org/array-api/latest/): array semantics.
- [NumPy broadcasting](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.broadcasting.html): future broadcasting rules.
- [Netlib BLAS](https://www.netlib.org/blas/): future linear algebra conventions.
- [tinygrad architecture](https://docs.tinygrad.org/developer/developer/): conceptual execution stages only.
- [cppreference](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp) and the C++ standard: language
  and standard-library behavior.
